   "reasoning": "The customer says their site is published and not password protected and reports it is not indexed. They want to know why and how to fix it. The excerpts together state the checks and actions to take: how to confirm indexing (site: search) and that publishing is required [1][10]; that password-protected or members-only pages return 403 and won\u2019t be indexed [5][4][6]; that Google may discover but defer indexing and suggest using the URL Inspection tool and Request Indexing [3][2][7]; that a \u2018noindex\u2019 directive or robots.txt rules can prevent indexing and how to address them [4][7][9]; and that verifying with Google Search Console and submitting a sitemap can help speed indexing [6][8][10]. None of these required facts are uniquely held by the customer (we don\u2019t need their domain to give the documented troubleshooting steps), and the customer is not asking us to act on their account. Therefore we can RESOLVE using the KB steps. The answer will list the corrective steps with citations to the supporting excerpts.",
   "answer": "Here are the documented steps to troubleshoot and try to get your site indexed by Google:\n\n1) Confirm indexing and that the site is published: search \"site:yourdomain\" to see what pages Google has indexed; ensure the site and page are published (search engines can only index published pages) [10][1].\n\n2) Re-check access restrictions: password-protected pages or members-only pages return a 403 and will not be indexed, so make sure the homepage and the pages you want indexed are public [5][4][6].\n\n3) Check for a noindex directive or robots rules: Google can report a Submitted URL Marked 'noindex' or pages blocked by robots.txt; remove any unintended \"noindex\" meta tag or update robots.txt if you want the page indexed [4][7][9]. Note that some Wix pages (e.g. certain blog archive/tag pages or form pages) have noindex by default [9].\n\n4) Use Google Search Console tools: inspect the URL with Google's URL Inspection Tool and, if everything looks OK, click Request Indexing; the URL Inspection Tool also helps diagnose crawl/render issues [2][3].\n\n5) Verify and reconnect Search Console if needed: verify your site ownership in Google Search Console and reconnect/refresh authorization if Wix lost connection to Search Console (this can block verification/indexing steps) [6][8].\n\n6) Submit your sitemap: after verifying the site you can submit your sitemap directly in Google Search Console to help Google crawl and categorize your site faster [8][10].\n\n7) Consider content quality and duplicate content: if Google shows \"Discovered - currently not indexed\" it may be delaying crawl due to site load or because the page\u2019s content is low quality/duplicate; improve unique page content and structure so each page has distinct, valuable text [3][7].\n\n8) Wait and retry: indexing can take time and is controlled by Google; if you\u2019ve fixed issues, request indexing and allow some time for Google to recrawl [1][8][3].\n\nIf you follow those steps and the page still isn\u2019t indexed, tell me whether Google Search Console reports any specific status or error message (for example: \"Submitted URL marked 'noindex'\", \"Discovered - currently not indexed\", a 403/401, or crawl errors) and I will point to the next KB-backed steps for that exact case [4][3][5][7].",
   "v4_decision": "RESOLVE",
   "reference_answer": "Please follow these steps:\n1. Make sure that search engines can index your site\nYou can choose whether or not to allow search engines to index your site in your [SEO Settings] (https://support.wix.com/en/article/customizing-your-seo-settings-1420929). It's important to let search engines index your site if you want it to appear in search engine results. \n\n1. Go to your [SEO Dashboard] (https://manage.wix.com/my-account/site-selector/?siteType=HtmlWeb&actionUrl=https://www.wix.com/dashboard/{{metaSiteId}}/seo-home?referralInfo=KB-f3388913-140f-47a9-b2ba-4bf86b60e04e&title=Select%20a%20Site&buttonText=Go+to+SEO).\n2. Select Go to SEO Settings under Tools and settings.\n3. Make sure the Let search engines index your site toggle is enabled.\n\n2. Check the index status of your page\nIf you're not sure about the index status of a page, you can use [Google's URL Inspection tool] (https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/9012289) or [the Wix Site Inspection tool] (https://support.wix.com/en/article/wix-site-inspection-an-overview) to check it. These tools can tell you if your page is indexed or if there are any issues that are preventing it from being indexed.\n\n3. Check if your pages has any redirects\nWix's URL Redirect Manager lets you create 301 redirects for pages on your site. 301 redirects tell web browsers that the URL of a page on your site has changed permanently to a new URL. It also tells browsers that visitors who try to access the page's old URL should be sent to the new URL instead.\n\nIf your page has a 301 redirect and the page doesn't appear in search results, try deleting the redirect.\n\n1. [Go to your SEO Dashboard] (https://manage.wix.com/my-account/site-selector/?siteType=HtmlWeb&actionUrl=https://www.wix.com/dashboard/{{metaSiteId}}/seo-home?referralInfo=KB-f3388913-140f-47a9-b2ba-4bf86b60e04e&title=Select%20a%20Site&buttonText=Go+to+SEO).\n2. Select Go to URL Redirect Manager under Tools and settings.\n3. Hover over the 301 redirect that you want to delete.\n4. Click the Delete icon . \n\n4. Submit Sitemap: Make sure your sitemap is submitted to Google. Wix automatically submits your sitemap, but you can also do it [manually via Google Search Console] (https://support.wix.com/en/article/submitting-your-sitemap-and-urls-directly-to-search-engines).\n\nHowever, search engines cannot guarantee that every page of your site will be indexed. It's important to note that this is perfectly normal in many cases. \n",
