   "injection_detected": false,
   "policy_override": null,
   "reasoning": "The customer wants to know why an automated email sent after a form submission isn't being delivered, and says they've already checked the automation statistics. The excerpts together state the troubleshooting checklist and possible causes I would tell them to check: spam folder and whitelisting, that the contact is active/subscribed, email validity, that the automation's frequency/timing settings (including 'once per contact' / 'Trigger once per person') are not preventing sends, that the correct trigger app and specific form are selected, that the form contains and maps a proper Email field synced to Contacts, and to test while logged out. None of these facts are unique to the customer's account (no site name, form name, or contact address was required to give the checklist), so no customer-only fact is missing. A human action on the account is not required because all steps are self-service troubleshooting. The KB excerpts fully state the checks and remedies, so I can RESOLVE the ticket by giving the documented checklist and next steps along with citations.",
   "answer": "Here\u2019s a step-by-step checklist of documented checks to troubleshoot an automation that triggered on form submission but didn\u2019t send the email. I\u2019ve included the KB citations for each item. 1) Confirm spam folder and whitelist sender addresses \u2014 automated emails can land in spam; whitelist no-reply@test.wixshoutout.com and the automations sender addresses as instructed [1][2]. 2) Verify the recipient contact is active/subscribed \u2014 automation emails won\u2019t send to inactive contacts; look for the SUBSCRIBED label on the contact card [1][2]. 3) Check the recipient email is valid \u2014 invalid addresses won\u2019t receive mail even if the automation triggered [1]. 4) Check automation timing and frequency \u2014 ensure the timing/delay is correct and that Limit frequency is not set to Once per contact (or Trigger once per person) if you expect repeated sends; changing timing can also reset pending actions [5][2][1][4]. 5) Make sure the correct trigger app and the correct form are selected for the automation (Wix Forms must be the trigger app and, if you chose a specific form, confirm it\u2019s the right one) [5][3][7]. 6) Ensure the form includes an Email field and that that field is the proper Email field type \u2014 sending an email action requires an email field [3][4][7]. 7) Confirm the form\u2019s email field is synced to your Contacts list (Email or Email (Main)); if it isn\u2019t, a contact won\u2019t be created and the automated email won\u2019t send [3][4][8]. 8) If you hold multiple email addresses for the same contact, emails may be sent to an alternate address \u2014 check the contact card for multiple emails [3][4]. 9) When testing, make sure you are logged out of your Wix account before submitting the form (being logged in can merge test addresses with your login and affect sends) [3][10]. 10) If you\u2019ve checked all of the above and the automation statistics show the automation triggered but no delivery, capture a screenshot or video of the steps and contact support with that evidence so they can investigate further [10]. If you\u2019d like, tell me which of these checks you\u2019ve already done (for example: spam, email field present and synced, timing/frequency settings) and any results you saw, and I\u2019ll point to the next specific step.",
   "v4_decision": "RESOLVE",
   "reference_answer": "If your automation is triggering but the automated email isn't sent, follow these steps:\n\nStep 1 | Check the spam folder\nWhen you set up an automation to send an email to your contact, the email could end up in their spam folder. They (or you, if you're testing the automation) should go to their inbox and look for the email in that folder as well. Learn more about automations sent through Wix Email Marketing being marked as spam.\n\nIf checking the spam folder doesn't solve the issue, please whitelist the following emails:\n- no-reply@test.wixshoutout.com\n- If you have not set a confirmed reply-to email: @automations.ascendbywix.com\n- If you have set a confirmed reply-to email: Your email address.\n\nStep 2 | Check that the recipient's email address is active\nCheck that the recipient's email address is active and that they've subscribed to your mailing list. If they have, you'll see a \"SUBSCRIBED\" label on their contact card. If your automation sends an email to an inactive contact, the email will not go through.\n\nStep 3 | Check that the recipient's email address is valid\nCheck that the recipient\u2019s email address is valid. If it's not, the recipient won't receive an email (even if the automation was set up correctly).\n\nStep 4 |  Check that the correct trigger app is set: Ensure that your trigger app is set to Wix Forms, not Site Members or any other app.\n  \nStep 5 | Verify the correct form is selected: If you've set a specific form to trigger your automation (rather than any form), make sure you've selected the right form.\n  \nStep 6 |  Ensure there is an email/phone field in your form: If your automation's trigger is \"Form submitted,\" it relies on specific contact details to complete the action. If these contact details are missing, the action cannot be completed.\n  \nStep 7 |  Check that the email field is the correct form field type: Access the form in your Editor to manage its fields. Hover over your email field to ensure its title is \"Email Field.\"\n  \nStep 8 |  Verify that the email field is synced with an email field in your Contact List: The email field in your form needs to be synced with your Contact List. The synced field must be either \"Email\" or \"Email (Main).\" If it\u2019s anything else, it won't work as an email field. Without this sync, a contact is not created, and an automated email cannot be sent.\n  \nStep 9 |  Check if you hold multiple email addresses for one contact: Access the contact's contact card to see if you have multiple email addresses for them. If a contact has multiple email addresses, automated emails are usually sent to an additional email rather than the contact's main/primary email. Contacts with only one email address are not affected.",
